course: dictionary
lesson: 10842
title: Verse
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Verse can mean poetry writing, with lines that often have a rhythm and rhyme. «The last verse asks: "Who were the first to cry Nowell?"» Here a verse is a group of lines in a song or poem, and it is one part of the whole song.
explain: A song often has verses and a chorus. The verses tell the story, and the chorus comes back again and again. «The song has four verses in all.» So if you count the verses, you count the groups of lines before the chorus, not the chorus itself.
explain: In the Bible, a verse is one short numbered part of a chapter. People find a Bible verse by its book, chapter and verse number. When someone says «chapter and verse», they mean all the exact details.
explain: Poems can also be free verse. «Free verse has no regular rhyme or beat, but the poet breaks the lines where the reader should pause.» So the words still form poetry, even without a neat pattern.
fact: Verse is writing in the form of poetry, with lines and often rhythm and rhyme.
fact: A verse is a group of lines in a poem or song. Another word for it is stanza.
fact: A Bible verse is one short numbered part of a chapter.
fact: «Free verse» is poetry with no regular rhyme or beat.
fact: «Chapter and verse» means all the exact details.
exercise: «The song has four verses in all.» How many verses does the song have? | Six | Ten | Two | *Four
exercise: «Free verse has no regular rhyme or beat, but the poet breaks the lines where the reader should pause.» Free verse has no regular what? | Poets | Line breaks | *Rhyme or beat | Imagery
exercise: Bible verses are found by book, chapter and what? | *Verse number | Date of printing | Colour | Page size
test: In the French song «Alouette», each verse adds what? | A day of the week | *A body part | A name | A colour
test: What does «chapter and verse» mean? | A kind of rhyme | A type of song | *All the exact details | A long, boring story
exam: What is a verse in a song? | A single word repeated over and over | A printed book cover | A drum beat | *A group of lines, often followed by a chorus
